# Blocks

Each block type is a Lua file: `assets/block/<namespace>/<name>.lua` is the block `<namespace>:<name>`, so `worldcraft/stone.lua` is `worldcraft:stone`. A file returns a table of the fields below, written in Luau (see `assets/item/README.md`: comments, local values, and shared helpers from `assets/lib/`). A key that isn't a plain name is written `["worldcraft:water"] = ...`. Families made of one material share a helper: `require("worldcraft:blocks")` makes stairs and slabs. The game reads every file when it starts; a file with a problem is left out, and each problem is logged with its file, line and column.

```lua
-- Pockets of it in stone; breaks into coal.
return {
  flags = {"solid", "opaque"},
  textures = "worldcraft:coal_ore",
  hardness = 2.0,
  mineable = "pickaxe",
  drops = {{item = "worldcraft:coal", count = {1, 4}}},
}
```

Only `textures` is required. Leave out anything that's the default.

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `about` | A note for whoever reads the file; the game ignores it. |
| `flags` | Any of: `solid` (collides), `opaque` (a full cube that hides faces and blocks light), `cutout` (holes in the texture), `casts_ao` (darkens nearby corners), `culls_self` (no faces between two of it, like glass), `translucent` (blended, like water), `replaceable` (placing a block replaces it), `fluid`, `needs_support` (breaks without the block it rests on), `neighbour_updates` (gets a block update when a neighbour changes), `updates_when_set` (gets a block update the tick after it's set, too: fire). |
| `light` | Light it gives off, 0â€“15. |
| `snuffed` | For a block that gives light: the name of the block it becomes when a monster puts it out (`worldcraft:torch_snuffed`), keeping its axes (which wall a torch is on). Debris flies, but nothing drops. Without it, monsters leave the light alone. They also leave alone one with too little sky light, such as one deep in a cave (see the `snuff` row in `assets/mob/README.md`). While a monster is coming to snuff it, a torch's flame gutters (`particles`) and a lamp flickers (`components`). |
| `absorbs` | Extra light levels lost passing through it, 0â€“15. |
| `shape` | `cube` (the default); a shape in `assets/shape/`, named with its namespace (`assets/shape/README.md`): `worldcraft:cross` (plants), `worldcraft:post` (torches), `worldcraft:slab`, `worldcraft:stairs`, `worldcraft:snow_layer` (the bottom 2/16 of the cell: snow lying on a block), `worldcraft:fence` (a post, with rails out to the sides it's joined on: give it `connects`), `worldcraft:panel` (a board one pixel thick against its support: ladders), `worldcraft:rope` (a strand two pixels thick down the middle, tied to a wall it hangs on; rope can hang from its bottom), `worldcraft:table` (a board across the top on four legs, colliding as the whole cell), `worldcraft:platform` (a board two pixels thick across the top of the cell, braced by two diagonal struts on a wall beside it when there's a platform opposite the wall, or it has fewer than two platforms beside it; it only stops what comes down onto it, so it can be jumped up through and walked under, and crouching on it drops through it, holding crouch landing on the next one down) (give it `components = {"braced"}`), `worldcraft:door` (a panel 3/16 thick, a part of a multi-part block; it needs `parts` and `components = {"open"}`); or `fire`, which the game draws itself, by what's around it: on a floor, flames round the edges; otherwise a sheet of flame up each wall beside it, and four sloping just under a ceiling. |
| `placed` | How placing it picks a state: `oriented` (lies along the face it's placed against, like logs: `name[oriented=z]`, `x` or `y`), `attach` (on the floor or a wall, like torches: `name[attach=floor]`, or the wall's side, `east`, `west`, `north` or `south`) or `facing` (faces the way the player looks, like stairs and furnaces: `name[facing=east]`). |
| `connects` | `true` to join the blocks beside it, like fences: solid full blocks, and others of its own type. It has a state for each set of sides it's joined on (`name[east,north]`), kept up to date as the blocks around it change. |
| `parts` | The cells it fills, if more than one (doors, beds): `{{textures = ...}, {offset = {0, 0, 1}, textures = ...}}`, the first where it's placed. Each `offset` is from the first part, in the block's own frame: x forward (the way it faces, if it's placed `facing`; east if not), y to its left, z up. A part's `textures` (as above, any faces) replace the block's. It has a state for each part (`name[facing=north,part=1]`); it's placed whole or not at all, setting or breaking one part takes the others with it, and it drops once. |
| `support` | With `needs_support`: `below` rests on the block under it; `any` clings to any solid block beside it, above or below (fire), and breaks when none is left. |
| `textures` | One texture for every face, or `{side = ..., top = ..., bottom = ...}`, or each face by name (`east`, `west`, `north`, `south`, `top`, `bottom`). A block placed `facing` can also have a `front` (the face turned back towards whoever placed it: a furnace's door), and a `front_lit`: then it has a lit state of each facing (`name[facing=east,lit]`) with that front, which the game lights (a furnace with heat). A texture `worldcraft:stone` is `assets/texture/worldcraft/stone.png`. |
| `lit_light` | Light it gives off while lit (a `front_lit` block), 0â€“15. |
| `tint` | `{red, green, blue}` (0 to 1), multiplied with the texture. |
| `fluid` | `{spread = 7, tick_delay = 5, infinite = true, reaction = "worldcraft:stone", flowing_texture = "worldcraft:water_flow", current = 1.5}`: flows `spread` blocks, updating every `tick_delay` ticks; two sources make a third if `infinite`; another fluid flowing into it makes `reaction`. `"flowing_texture"` is what its flowing and falling blocks show (the source keeps `textures`), so still and moving fluid can look and animate differently. `"current"` is how fast its flow carries the player, mobs and items with any part of them in it, in blocks a second, along the way it flows (away from a source, or from where it pours down, and out over edges); the default is 0. |
| `fog` | Seen from inside it: `{color = {r, g, b}, distance = 24, dims_at_night = true}`. |
| `particles` | What it gives off from its tip (the top of a torch): `{flame = <sprites>, guttering = <sprites>, smoke = <sprites>}`, any of them (`guttering` only with a `flame`). Sprites are `{texture = "worldcraft:smoke", pixels = 8, frames = 8, color = "#131313"}`: a sheet of `frames` (default 1) square sprites of `pixels` texels, left to right, drawn at the block's texel size (16 to a block), tinted `color` (`"#rrggbb"` or `{r, g, b}`; default white). Smoke also has `"per_second"`, puffs a second. A flame stays on the tip, flickering; smoke drifts up from it, going through its sprites from the last to the first. While a monster is coming to snuff it (see `snuffed`), the flame gutters: it plays its `guttering` sprites over and over (12 a second), leaning away from the monster, and its smoke blows away from it. The flame is drawn facing the camera, so it can only lean left or right on the screen: the sprites lean right, and they're mirrored to lean left. Only near the player. |
| `hardness` | Seconds to break by hand (0 breaks at once), or `"unbreakable"`. The default is 1. |
| `mineable` | The kind of tool that breaks it faster: items whose `mining` tags include it (`pickaxe`, `shovel` or `axe`; see `assets/item/README.md`). |
| `drops` | What breaking it drops, if not itself: `{}` for nothing, or items with a count or a `{least, most}` range. |
| `item` | `false` if there's no item for it (fluids, bedrock). |
| `placed_beside` | `true`: the player can only place it with a wall (a block that holds things up), or another of it, beside it: east, west, north or south (platforms). With `"on_update": "held_beside"` (and `neighbour_updates`), it breaks once it's no longer joined to a wall through others of its kind, each beside the next. |
| `climbable` | `true`: the player climbs it (ladders, rope): in its cell, jump climbs, crouch holds on, otherwise they slide slowly down, and a fall ends there unhurt. |
| `tags` | Words other rules look for: `log` (keeps leaves alive), `leaves`, `fluid_source`, `flammable` (fire spreads beside it, and keeps burning), `snow` (a block with `"components": ["snowy"]` under it is snowy), `table` (the heart of a room: room checks start from it; give it the `table` entity too), `door` (a door into a room, in its boundary). |
| `on_update` | A rule run on its block updates: `leaf_decay`, `held_beside` (with `placed_beside`: breaks once it isn't joined to a wall through others of its kind, each beside the next: give it `neighbour_updates`), `fall` (falls when the block below isn't solid, like sand: give it `neighbour_updates` and `updates_when_set`), `fire` (spreads to a cell near it beside something `flammable` now and then, and goes out, soon if nothing beside it is; see `src/worldcraft/fire.h`),, `braced_platform` (for platforms: as `held_beside`, and keeps its `"braced"` axes up to date with the walls beside it: give it `neighbour_updates` and `updates_when_set`), `pair` (keeps a `"pair"` block's pairing up to date: give it `neighbour_updates` and `updates_when_set`), or `snowy` (makes a `"snowy"` block snowy while a block tagged `snow` is on top of it: give it `neighbour_updates`). |
| `on_random_tick` | A rule run now and then at random, wherever the block is within simulation distance: each block gets a random tick about once every 30 seconds (`RANDOM_TICK_SECONDS`, `src/blocks/random_ticks.h`). Any `on_update` rule, `flicker` (with the `"flicker"` component: stops it flickering, unless a monster is coming to snuff it), or `spread` (looks at one cell of the 3x3x3 around it, diagonals too, picked at random; if that's its `spreads_to` block, with nothing opaque on top, it becomes this block, like grass over dirt; see `src/worldcraft/spread.h`). |
| `spreads_to` | The block that `"on_random_tick": "spread"` spreads onto: `"worldcraft:dirt"`. |
| `on_break` | A function run when it's broken, by anyone or anything (below): TNT's lights it, `ctx.block:explode()`. |
| `on_hit` | A function run each time the player swings at it (below): when they start hitting it, and every quarter second while they keep on. |
| `place` | A function run once the player has placed it (below). |
| `components` | The game's own components its type has, which can give it more states: `{"braced"}` (platforms: four axes, `braced_east` to `braced_south`, saying which walls beside it it is braced on; `assets/shape/worldcraft/platform.lua` draws its struts from them), `{"open"}` (opens and closes: `name[facing=east,open]`); `{"pair"}` (two side by side, east and west or north and south, each the other's only one of its kind, are drawn as one wide block, like a large chest: `name[facing=south,pair=east]`, `pair` the way to the other; keep it up to date with `"on_update": "pair"`. The two turn to face one way. Their long sides, top and bottom show each texture `T` as its halves `T_left` and `T_right`, seen from outside, and where `T` would run across the pair, as `T_left_turned` and `T_right_turned`, those turned a quarter clockwise; see `src/worldcraft/pairs.h`); `{"wall"}` (with `needs_support`: goes only against a wall, like ladders: `name[wall=east]`, the wall's side); `{"hang"}` (with `needs_support`: hangs under a block or is tied to the side of one, like rope: `name[hang=ceiling]`, or the side, `east`, `west`, `north` or `south`; placed on a block of its own kind that holds it up from below, it goes on the bottom of the line); `{"snowy"}` (looks different under snow, like grass: `name[snowy]`, whose sides show each side texture `T` as `T_snowy`; keep it up to date with `"on_update": "snowy"`); `{"flicker"}` (for a light with no flame, like a lamp: while a monster is coming to snuff it, it flickers, now and then showing its flickering state, `name[flickering]`, for a moment; that state gives the same light, and each face shows its texture `T` as `T_flicker`. Give it `"on_random_tick": "flicker"` too, which puts one left flickering, saved or unloaded while it flickered, back to normal; see `src/worldcraft/flicker.h`). The game registers them by name (`block_use.h`). |
| `use` | A function run when the player uses it, instead of using the held item (below): a crafting table's opens its menu, `ctx.block:open_menu("worldcraft:crafting_table")`. |
| `entity` | Give it a block entity, saved with its chunk, with these kinds of component: `{"crate"}` (counts how often it's opened), `{"furnace"}` (a furnace's slots and heat: see `assets/config/worldcraft/furnace.lua`; what it held drops when it's broken), `{"chest"}` (3 rows of 10 slots; two chests side by side, each the other's only chest neighbour, open together as one of 6 rows; what it held spills out when it's broken), `{"table"}` (the world's list of tables knows of it while it's there: placed, it's added; gone by any means, it's taken off, and an NPC whose room it was loses it). The game registers kinds by name (`block_use.h`). |
| `contact_damage` | Hit points it takes from the player or a mob touching it (at their feet or the block above), each time their hurt flash is over: lava's 4. |

## Functions

`use`, `place`, `on_break` and `on_hit` are functions, called with a `ctx`, as an item's `use` is (`assets/item/README.md`): tables of functions called with a colon, which work only while the function runs. One naming an action (`use = {action = "open_chest"}`, `on_break = "explosive"`), as files once did, is refused with a warning.

- `use(ctx)` runs when the player uses the block. It returns `true` if something happened; `false` (or nothing) uses the held item instead.
- `place(ctx)` runs once the player has placed the block (not when a block is set any other way).
- `on_break(ctx)` runs when the block is broken, by anyone or anything.
- `on_hit(ctx)` runs each time the player swings at it: a few times a second, while they hit it.

An error is logged, and does nothing else: a use is refused; a placing, breaking or hit still happens.

```lua
-- A door: opens and shuts, both parts together.
use = function(ctx)
  return ctx.block:toggle_open()
end,
```

| `ctx` | |
|---|---|
| `ctx.block:position()` | Its `x, y, z`. |
| `ctx.block:name()` | Its type's name (`worldcraft:door`). |
| `ctx.block:set(name)` | Make it another block, by name with its states (`"worldcraft:door[facing=east]"`). |
| `ctx.block:toggle_open()` | Open it if it's shut, shut it if it's open, all its parts together (it needs `components = {"open"}`). Only in `use`. |
| `ctx.block:open_menu(name)` | Open a menu (`assets/menu/README.md`) on it, once `use` is done (a menu file of your own opens like the game's; its `ctx.block` is this block): a furnace's slots are its own. `false` if what the menu shows isn't there (a furnace's menu on a block that isn't one). Only in `use`. |
| `ctx.block:open_container()` | Open its crate (`entity = {"crate"}`), which counts how often it's been opened; `false` if it isn't one. Only in `use`. |
| `ctx.block:partner()` | The `x, y, z` of the other block of its pair (`components = {"pair"}`: a double chest), or nothing. |
| `ctx.block:explode()` | Light it as TNT: it goes off a second later. |
| `ctx.block:broken_by_player()` | In `on_break`: whether the player broke it. |
| `ctx.player:...` | As for items: `health`, `max_health`, `heal`, `heal_over_time`. |
| `ctx.world:block(x, y, z)`, `:set_block(x, y, z, name)` | The name of the block there (`core:air`; `""` where it isn't loaded); set one (`false` if it can't be). |
| `ctx.world:message(text)`, `:time_of_day()`, `:is_night()`, `:days()` | As for items. |

`assets/types/worldcraft.d.luau` has every method, for an editor.
return {
  flags = {"solid", "opaque"},
  textures = "worldcraft:bedrock",
  hardness = "unbreakable",
  item = false,
}
return {
  flags = {"solid", "opaque", "needs_support"},
  support = "below",
  textures = {
    side = "worldcraft:cactus_side",
    top = "worldcraft:cactus_top",
    bottom = "worldcraft:cactus_top",
  },
  hardness = 0.4,
}
return {
  about = "Holds 3 rows of 10 stacks. Two side by side (each the other's only chest neighbour) open together as one large chest of 6 rows, and look like one. Placed facing whoever places it; broken, what it held spills out.",
  flags = {"solid", "opaque", "neighbour_updates", "updates_when_set"},
  placed = "facing",
  textures = {
    side = "worldcraft:chest_side",
    top = "worldcraft:chest_top",
    bottom = "worldcraft:chest_top",
    front = "worldcraft:chest_front",
  },
  components = {"pair"},
  on_update = "pair",
  hardness = 2.5,
  mineable = "axe",
  use = function(ctx)
    -- A double chest, if it's one of a pair.
    return ctx.block:open_menu(if ctx.block:partner() then "worldcraft:large_chest" else "worldcraft:chest")
  end,
  entity = {"chest"},
  tags = {"flammable"},
}
return {
  flags = {"solid", "opaque"},
  textures = "worldcraft:coal_ore",
  hardness = 2.0,
  mineable = "pickaxe",
  drops = {
    {
      item = "worldcraft:coal",
      count = {1, 4},
    },
  },
}
return {
  flags = {"solid", "opaque"},
  textures = {
    side = "worldcraft:crafting_side",
    top = "worldcraft:crafting_top",
    bottom = "worldcraft:planks",
  },
  hardness = 1.0,
  mineable = "axe",
  use = function(ctx)
    return ctx.block:open_menu("worldcraft:crafting_table")
  end,
  tags = {"flammable"},
}
return {
  flags = {"solid", "opaque"},
  textures = "worldcraft:crate",
  hardness = 1.0,
  mineable = "axe",
  use = function(ctx)
    return ctx.block:open_container()
  end,
  entity = {"crate"},
  tags = {"flammable"},
}
return {
  flags = {"cutout", "needs_support"},
