# Open air below this height is sea.
sea_level = 262
# Land up to this far above sea level is beach (sand).
beach_height = 2
# Mountains above this are snow-capped (except in deserts), with no trees.
snow_line = 400
# Caves below this height are flooded with lava.
lava_level = 64

# Land: rolling hills around land_base_z, up to hill_amplitude above or below
# it, and mountain ridges up to mountain_height higher in mountainous regions.
# The wavelengths are the sizes of the hills, of the ridges, and of the regions
# that have mountains.
land_base_z = 280
hill_amplitude = 48
hill_wavelength = 256
mountain_height = 220
mountain_wavelength = 256
mountain_region_wavelength = 48

# Continents and oceans: the size of continents, how gradually the land
# slopes down into the sea (bigger is a longer coast), and how far below sea
# level the ocean floor is.
continent_wavelength = 600
continent_shelf = 0.15
ocean_depth = 36

# Size of the climate regions that make biomes (forest, desert, moor, tundra).
temperature_wavelength = 400
humidity_wavelength = 400

# How far high ground bulges in and out, making overhangs (0 for none).
overhang_amplitude_max = 64

# Winding caves: bigger makes them wider and more common (0 for none).
tube_threshold = 0.012
# How many blocks of ground they keep over them: 4 or more keeps the surface
# whole, fewer lets them open onto it (above sea level).
cave_min_depth = 0
# Worm caves: tunnels that wind from a start and branch. worm_count start in
# each 32-block column on average, each about worm_length blocks long;
# worm_twist is how sharply they turn, worm_radius_min/max the range of
# their widths (blocks from the middle to the wall), and worm_branching the
# chance one splits in two.
worm_count = 0.5
worm_length = 160
worm_twist = 1
worm_radius_min = 1.5
worm_radius_max = 4
worm_branching = 0.5
# Their shapes, each 0 for none: the width swelling and pinching (how much,
# and the blocks between swellings); rough walls (how far they're pushed in
# and out in blocks, and the size of the bumps); and the cross section going
# from tall and narrow to wide and low (how much, and the blocks between).
worm_width_amplitude = 0.7
worm_width_wavelength = 18
worm_wall_amplitude = 1.5
worm_wall_wavelength = 5
worm_shape_amplitude = 0.8
worm_shape_wavelength = 35
# Huge caverns are carved between these heights, in some regions.
cavern_bottom = 110
cavern_top = 200
# Springs: this share of the stone in caves' walls (open to the cave on one
# side), at least spring_min_depth under the land's top, is a source that
# pours out into it (0 for none): lava below spring_lava_level, water above.
spring_chance = 0.003
spring_min_depth = 16
spring_lava_level = 150

# Sky islands float around island_base_z. island_wavelength is their size, and
# island_threshold (0-1) how rare they are: higher means fewer and smaller.
island_base_z = 640
island_wavelength = 200
island_threshold = 0.28
# Islands gather in archipelagos, spread over about archipelago_wavelength: a big
# main island in the middle, smaller ones around it, and open sky between.
# Higher archipelago_threshold (-1 to 1) means fewer, smaller main islands;
# archipelago_strength is how tightly the smaller islands gather round them
# (lower spreads them further; 0 doesn't gather them at all).
archipelago_wavelength = 500
archipelago_threshold = 0.5
archipelago_strength = 1.5
# Inlets cut into the islands, about island_inlet_wavelength blocks apart, so
# main islands are squiggly; higher island_inlet_threshold (-1 to 1) means
# fewer.
island_inlet_wavelength = 60
island_inlet_threshold = 0
# Orbiters: small islands round main islands' coasts, some about
# island_orbiter_below blocks below them, in their shadow, and some about
# island_orbiter_above above. island_orbiters is how many (0 for none).
island_orbiters = 0.2
island_orbiter_below = 30
island_orbiter_above = 60
# Island undersides taper island_taper blocks deeper for each block in from
# the coast, curving round to at most island_depth deep in the middle.
island_taper = 1
island_depth = 100
# On the islands: hills up to island_hill_height high (higher
# island_hill_threshold, -1 to 1, means rarer); caves through them that open
# on top and underneath (bigger island_cave_threshold is wider and more); the
# chance of a lake in each 128-block square with room for one; and the
# fraction of flat grass with a tree.
island_hill_height = 16
island_hill_threshold = 0.3
island_cave_threshold = 0.02
island_lakes = 0.6
island_trees = 0.02
# Streams from springs inland to waterfalls over islands' edges: the chance
# of one in each 96-block square.
island_streams = 0.7

# Fraction of suitable ground with a tree, in forests and in the copses of
# moors and tundra.
forest_trees = 0.025
copse_trees = 0.2
# The HUD

What's drawn over the world while playing is HUD layers: `assets/hud/<namespace>/<name>.lua` is the layer `<namespace>:<name>`. The menus config (`assets/config/worldcraft/menus.lua`) names those drawn, in order, as `hud = {...}`. A layer is a menu without a panel (`assets/menu/README.md`): the same components, values and styles, each placed from a point of the window. Nothing in it is clicked, and nothing in it runs while playing.

A layer file returns a table:

| Field | |
|---|---|
| `components` | Its components, in the order they're drawn. Each needs its `anchor`. |
| `hideable` | `false` if hiding the HUD (F1) doesn't hide it (the version). Default `true`. |
| `style` | How it's drawn: `"default"`, the only style there is so far. |
| `about` | A note on what it is. |

## Components

Those of menus (`assets/menu/README.md`), each with its `anchor`: a point of the window, and the same point of the component, which `at` moves it from (`anchor = "bottom", at = {0, -4}` is in the middle, 4 pixels above the bottom edge). The game's layer has a `ui.hotbar`, two `ui.meter`s (hearts, and bubbles while short of breath) and a `lines` for the debug overlay. A HUD's text is white unless it has a `color`.

## Values

Those menus have (`assets/menu/README.md`), and:

| Value | |
|---|---|
| `health`, `max_health` | The player's hit points, and their most. |
| `air`, `max_air` | The player's breath, and its most. |
| `version` | The game's version. |
| `debug`, `show_debug` | The debug overlay's lines, and whether it's on (F3). |

The nametags over NPCs, the red flash when hurt, the profile chart (F3), the crosshair and the command line are drawn by the game, not by layers.
local ui = require("worldcraft:menus")

return {
  about = "While playing: the hotbar, hearts and (short of breath) bubbles above it, and the debug overlay (F3). F1 hides it.",
  components = {
    ui.hotbar {anchor = "bottom", at = {0, -4}},
    ui.meter {anchor = "bottom", at = {-94, -51}, value = "health", max = "max_health", per = 2, halves = true,
      icon = "worldcraft:heart", empty_icon = "worldcraft:heart_empty"},
    ui.meter {anchor = "bottom", at = {94, -51}, align = "right", value = "air", max = "max_air", hide_full = true,
      icon = "worldcraft:bubble"},
    ui.lines {anchor = "top_left", at = {6, 6}, value = "debug", shown = "show_debug"},
  },
}
local ui = require("worldcraft:menus")

return {
  about = "The game's version, in the top left, always (F1 doesn't hide it).",
  hideable = false,
  components = {
    ui.text {anchor = "top_left", at = {4, 4}, value = "version", scale = 3, color = {1, 1, 1}, shadow = true},
  },
}
# Items

Each item that isn't a block is a Lua file: `assets/item/<namespace>/<name>.lua` is the item `<namespace>:<name>`, so `worldcraft/stone_pickaxe.lua` is `worldcraft:stone_pickaxe`. Every block with an item (see `assets/block/README.md`) is an item too, without a file: it stacks to 64 and places its block. The game reads every file when it starts; a file with a problem is left out, and each problem is logged with its file (and line, for an error running it).

A file returns a table of the fields below. It's Luau (luau.org), run in a sandbox: it can have comments, local values, and shared helpers from `assets/lib/<namespace>/` (`require("worldcraft:door")`). A key that isn't a plain name is written `["worldcraft:water"] = ...`.

```lua
-- Scoops up a water or lava source.
return {
  texture = "worldcraft:bucket",
  stack = 1,
  use = {
    action = "scoop_fluid",
    fluids = {["worldcraft:water"] = "worldcraft:water_bucket", ["worldcraft:lava"] = "worldcraft:lava_bucket"},
  },
}
```

Only `texture` is required. Leave out anything that's the default.

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `about` | A note for whoever reads the file; the game ignores it. |
| `name` | The name shown to the player. The default comes from the file name: `water_bucket` is "Water bucket". |
| `texture` | Its icon: `worldcraft:bucket` is `assets/texture/worldcraft/bucket.png`. |
| `stack` | How many fit in one inventory slot, 1 to 65535. The default is 64. |
| `damage` | Hit points it takes off a mob when hitting with it. The default is a fist's, 4. |
| `mining` | `{tags = {"pickaxe"}, speed = 5}`: it breaks a block whose `mineable` is one of `tags` `speed` times faster than a hand. |
| `use` | What using it (right click) does: a function (below). Without it, using it does nothing. |
| `cooldown` | Seconds after using it before it can be used again (the wand's is 0.3). |

## Use

`use` is a function, called when the player uses the item, with a `ctx`: what it can see and change, as tables of functions called with a colon. It returns `true` if the item was used (its cooldown, the file's `cooldown`, starts), and `false` (or nothing) if not. An error is logged, and the use refused, the stack as it was. A `use` naming an action (`{action = "shoot"}`), as files once did, is refused with a warning.

```lua
-- A water bucket: pours a source of water where it's pointed, and is empty.
use = function(ctx)
  if not ctx.item:place("worldcraft:water") then
    return false
  end
  ctx.item:become("worldcraft:bucket")
  return true
end,
```

What an item's use has:

| `ctx` | |
|---|---|
| `ctx.player:health()`, `:max_health()` | Its hit points now, and at most. |
| `ctx.player:heal(n)` | Heal `n` at once, up to full: the hit points healed (0 if dead or at full health). Food. |
| `ctx.player:heal_over_time(n, s)` | Heal `n` evenly over `s` seconds; being hurt doesn't stop it. `false` if refused: dead, at full health, or healing already with as much left to give (a Bandage on a Bandage). |
| `ctx.player:shoot(projectile)` | Shoot a projectile (`worldcraft:fireball`; `assets/projectile/README.md`) the way the player looks. |
| `ctx.player:check_room()` | The clipboard: check the room pointed into (from the cell in front of the face pointed at, its table, then its rules), saying in chat whether it suits an NPC, and if not, the first rule it fails. `false` if nothing's pointed at. |
| `ctx.item:name()`, `:count()` | The stack used: its item's name (`worldcraft:bandage`), and how many. |
| `ctx.item:take(n)` | Use up `n` (1 if left out); how many are left. |
| `ctx.item:place(block)` | Put a block, or a fluid's source, where it's pointed, as a held block is placed (not inside the player, and only where it can go); `false` if it isn't. |
| `ctx.item:become(item)` | Turn the stack into another item. |
| `ctx.item:scoop_fluid({[fluid] = item, ...})` | Take up a source of one of the fluids named that's pointed at, and become the item named for it; `false` if there's none. |
| `ctx.aim:hit()`, `:block()`, `:face()` | Whether a block's pointed at; its `x, y, z`; and the way the face pointed at faces. |
| `ctx.world:message(text)` | Say something in chat. |
| `ctx.world:time_of_day()`, `:is_night()`, `:days()` | 0 to 1 from midnight; whether the stars are out; days the world has run. |
| `ctx.world:block(x, y, z)`, `:set_block(x, y, z, block)` | The name of the block there; set one. |

`assets/types/worldcraft.d.luau` has every `ctx` table and method, for an editor (luau-lsp). The `ctx` works only while the function runs: one kept and used later is an error. Uses are for things the player does now and then, not every frame: in a Debug build, one called more than 20 times a second, or taking over a millisecond, is warned about. `assets/lib/worldcraft/healing.lua` has the healing items' uses (`require("worldcraft:healing")`).

## The starter kit

What a new player starts with is `assets/config/worldcraft/starter_kit.lua`: a list of `{item = ..., count = ...}`. They are added in order, into the hotbar first.

## The inventory's size

`assets/config/worldcraft/inventory.lua` says how many slots the inventory has: `hotbar` (1 to 10 slots, a key each) and `backpack` (the rows above it, in the inventory menu: the `hotbar` and `backpack` containers, `assets/menu/README.md`). A world saved with another size keeps what it can: each stack in its slot if there still is one, otherwise in an empty slot.
local healing = require("worldcraft:healing")

return {
  name = "Bandage",
  texture = "worldcraft:bandage",
  stack = 15,
  cooldown = 0.5,
  -- 5 hit points over 15 seconds.
  use = healing.over_time(5, 15),
}
return {
  texture = "worldcraft:bone",
}
return {
  texture = "worldcraft:bucket",
  stack = 1,
  -- Takes up a source of water or lava pointed at, and is full of it.
  use = function(ctx)
    return ctx.item:scoop_fluid({
      ["worldcraft:water"] = "worldcraft:water_bucket",
      ["worldcraft:lava"] = "worldcraft:lava_bucket",
    })
  end,
}
return {
  name = "Clipboard",
  texture = "worldcraft:clipboard",
  stack = 1,
  cooldown = 0.5,
  -- Says whether the room pointed into suits an NPC, and if not, why.
  use = function(ctx)
    return ctx.player:check_room()
  end,
}
return {
  texture = "worldcraft:coal",
}
return {
