local ui = require("worldcraft:menus")

-- A number setting, and buttons stepping it down and up.
local function stepper(y, format, setting, step)
  return ui.column {at = {8, y}, children = {
    ui.text {value = setting, format = format},
    ui.row {gap = 4, children = {
      ui.button {size = {26, 18}, label = "<", on_click = function(ctx) ctx.settings:step(setting, -step) end},
      ui.button {size = {26, 18}, label = ">", on_click = function(ctx) ctx.settings:step(setting, step) end},
    }},
  }}
end

return {
  about = "Esc: the view distance, field of view and mouse speed; save; quit.",
  title = "Settings",
  size = {280, 250},
  components = {
    stepper(28, "View: {} chunks", "view_distance", 1),
    stepper(68, "FOV: {}", "fov", 5),
    stepper(108, "Mouse: {:.2f}", "mouse_sensitivity", 0.01),
    ui.column {at = {8, 152}, gap = 4, children = {
      ui.button {size = {264, 22}, label = "Resume", on_click = function(ctx) ctx.menu:close() end},
      ui.button {size = {264, 22}, label = "Save world", on_click = function(ctx) ctx.game:save() end},
      ui.button {size = {264, 22}, label = "Save and quit", on_click = function(ctx) ctx.game:quit() end},
    }},
  },
}
local ui = require("worldcraft:menus")
return {
  components = {
    ui.inventory(),
    ui.panel {anchor = "top_right", at = {-40, 40}, padding = 6, children = {
      ui.text {value = "npc.full_name"},
      ui.slots {at = {8, 26}, container = "shop", columns = 10},
      ui.button {size = {72, 18}, label = "Talk", on_click = function(ctx)
        ctx.npc:talk()
      end},
    }},
    ui.hotbar {anchor = "bottom", at = {0, -4}},
  },
}
# Mobs

Each mob type is a Lua file: `assets/mob/<namespace>/<name>.lua` is the mob `<namespace>:<name>`, so `worldcraft/cow.lua` is `worldcraft:cow`. A file returns a table of the fields below, written in Luau (see `assets/item/README.md`: comments, local values, and shared helpers from `assets/lib/`). A key that isn't a plain name is written `["worldcraft:water"] = ...`. The game reads every file when it starts. A file with a problem is left out, and each problem is logged with its file, line and column.

Every mob wanders: it walks a while in a random direction, stands a while, and repeats. It turns back at cliffs and water, and jumps up steps.

```lua
-- Comes out of the dark at night, and fades away when day comes.
return {
  radius = 0.3, height = 1.95, walk_speed = 1.4, health = 20,
  drops = {{item = "worldcraft:bone", count = {1, 2}}},
  model = { ... },
  spawn = {{when = "night", light_max = 7, distance = {20, 40}, cap = 10, cap_radius = 64, fade_by_day = true}},
}
```

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `about` | A note for whoever reads the file; the game ignores it. |
| `radius`, `height` | Its collision box: half its width, and its height, in blocks. |
| `walk_speed` | Blocks per second (slower in water). |
| `health` | Hit points; it dies at 0. A fist takes 4. |
| `drops` | What it drops when it dies: items with a count or a `{least, most}` range, as a block's `drops`. Leave it out for nothing. |
| `coins` | The coins it drops when the player kills it (by hand, with a projectile, or with an explosive they lit): `{least, most}`, in copper (`assets/config/worldcraft/currency.lua`), rolled and dropped as one stack per coin (2 silver and 15 copper). Killed by anything else, it drops none. Leave it out for none, as passive mobs do; give rarer monsters more. |
| `model` | What it looks like, below. Required. |
| `spawn` | Where and when it appears, below. Without it, it only appears where something places it (the demo `spawn` command). |
| `attack` | It's hostile: `{"damage": 3, "reach": 0.9, "cooldown": 1.2, "sight": 24, "pursue": 40}`. When the player is within `sight` blocks (and roughly level with it), it goes for them instead of wandering, and keeps on until they are further than `pursue` blocks (at least `sight`; a hill of up to 8 blocks doesn't shake it off); when they're within `reach` of its body, it swings its arms (its `arm_a` and `arm_b` parts) and hits them for `damage` hit points, at most once every `cooldown` seconds. If there is no way to them at all (a wall, glass, a shut door), it patrols instead: it walks about the place it is in, standing now and then, until a way opens. If there is no way to them at all (a wall, glass, a shut door), it patrols instead: it walks about the place it is in, standing now and then, until a way opens. Leave it out for a mob that doesn't attack. |
| `glide` | How far it glides across nothing, in blocks (default 0). Walking off a ledge, it keeps its height instead of falling until it has moved this far across; if there's ground by then (up to this many empty cells across, at its level or one lower), it lands and walks on, and when it plans a route to the player it counts such a gap as a way across. Otherwise it falls. It turns back from a drop it can't glide across, as other mobs do from any drop. Standing on something starts it afresh. |
| `pushes` | `false`: it passes through the player and other kinds of mob, neither pushing nor being pushed, but still bumps into others of its own kind (and collides with blocks). The default is `true`. |
| `ignores_fluids` | `true`: water and lava don't affect it: it doesn't swim, sink or float, isn't carried by currents, and floats across their surface as across ground; lava doesn't burn it. The default is `false`. |
| `fall_damage` | `false`: falling doesn't hurt it. The default is `true`. |
| `trail` | It leaves smoke at its feet as it moves: `{texture = "worldcraft:smoke", pixels = 8, frames = 8, color = {240, 240, 245}, per_block = 2}`. `texture` is a sheet of `frames` sprites of `pixels` pixels (the one a torch's smoke uses, `assets/texture/worldcraft/smoke.png`, is white, so `color` tints it), and `per_block` puffs are left for each block it walks. The puffs drift up and shrink away, lit by the light where they are. Leave it out for none. |
| `snuff` | It puts out lights: `{range = 8, cooldown = 3, detour = 6}`. A light is a block that gives light and has a `snuffed` block in its file (`assets/block/README.md`). A mob with a clear line to one (glass stops the line) within `range` blocks walks as near the light as it can get and snuffs it, no more often than every `cooldown` seconds: the light becomes its `snuffed` block, with a burst of debris. Without a clear line, but seeing the light through blocks that light passes through (glass, leaves: any that aren't `opaque`), if it can walk round the light, to a cross round it, it goes as near the light as it can on the far side and snuffs it; it needn't see the light from there, or from either side. The cross lies along the grid: its far arm is whichever of east, west, north and south is most nearly straight on through the light from the mob, and its other two arms are square to that, to the light's left and right. The mob must be able to reach a cell on each arm: in the light's own row or column, that way out from it (at any height), and within `range` of it. So a light sunk in a wall, or in a niche, with no way to one of those lines, stays lit. A light with anything opaque between it and the mob, the mob leaves alone. So is a light in too little sky light, whatever the time of day (in its own cell, or for a block light doesn't go into, such as a lamp, beside its faces): by default, one with none at all, such as one deep in a cave (`snuff_sky_light_min` in `assets/config/worldcraft/mobs.cfg`, the same for every mob). Sky light that comes in at a cave mouth, or along the sky rays, counts. While it's after the player, it only goes for a light on the way: one that takes it no more than `detour` blocks (default 6) further than going straight for the player. Leave it out for a mob that leaves lights alone. |
| `avoids_sunlight` | `true`: it keeps out of sunlight. Sunlight is wherever it would start to fade away: by day, where the sky light at its head is above its `night` rule's `light_max` (see `fade_by_day`, below), so it needs a `night` rule with `fade_by_day`. In the shade, it won't step into sunlight: wandering, it turns back at the edge of a shadow or a cave mouth, as at a cliff; going for the player, it goes only by ways in the shade, and if the player is in the sun, it comes to the shade nearest them and waits there; going for a light, only by a way in the shade. One that's already in the sun carries on as it would anyway. At night there's no sunlight, so it makes no difference. The default is `false`. |

## Model

A model is a set of boxes, textured from a 64Ã—64 skin and coloured from a palette, or else shapes made in a 3D editor (Blockbench), in an OBJ file.

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `skin` | The 64Ã—64 texture its parts' nets are in: `worldcraft:skin_cow` is `assets/texture/worldcraft/skin_cow.png`. |
| `scale` | Modelled at this size, drawn this much bigger or smaller. The default is 1. |
| `stride` | How far its limbs swing while it walks, in radians. |
| `graze` | How far its head goes down while it stands still, in radians (a cow's 0.7). The default is 0. |
| `obj` | A model from an OBJ file instead of boxes: `"worldcraft:ghost"` is `assets/mob/worldcraft/ghost.obj`. Its objects are the model's parts, textured by the faces' own texture coordinates on the 64Ã—64 skin (which can have see-through texels: it's a cutout). It's Y up and faces +X, in blocks, with its feet at the origin, as Blockbench exports it. An object that isn't closed (a flat sheet) is drawn from both sides. `parts` is then optional: each entry is the name of one of the file's objects, with only the `color` (default the first), `pivot` and `swing` it has; `from`, `to` and `uv` aren't used. |
| `colors` | Up to 8: `{name = "coat", options = {{r, g, b}, ...}, paint = {r, g, b}}`, each 0â€“255. Each mob picks one of each colour's options, from its variant: the k-th colour with more than one option picks option `(variant >> 4k) % n`. `paint` is the colour `gen_mob_skins.py` paints the placeholder skin; it defaults to the first option. |
| `parts` | The boxes, below. |

Each part is a box in model space: x forward, y left, z up, with the origin at the feet.

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `name` | What it is (`left leg`), for whoever reads the file. |
| `from`, `to` | Opposite corners, `{x, y, z}`. |
| `color` | The name of one of `colors`. |
| `uv` | Where its net starts in the skin, `{x, y}` in pixels. A box `w`Ã—`d`Ã—`h` blocks (x, y, z) is 16 pixels a block. The net has two rows. The top row is `d` high, with the top at `w` across and the bottom at `2w`. The second row is `h` high, with the faces right, front, left and back starting at 0, `w`, `w + d` and `2w + d`. |
| `pivot` | The point it swings about, `{x, y, z}`. The default is `{0, 0, 0}`. |
| `swing` | `none` (the default); `stride_a` or `stride_b`, which swing with the walk in opposition (one leg forward, the other back); `head`, which goes down by `graze` while it stands; or `arm_a` or `arm_b`, which swing as `stride_a` and `stride_b` and also reach forward when it attacks. |

## Spawn rules

`spawn` is a list of rules, each with a `when`:

| `when` | Fields | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `generation` | `one_in`, `group`, and `biomes` or `biomes_except` | When the world generates a chunk column, one column in `one_in` has a group of `{least, most}` of them. They stand on dry ground with room for their height, and not on leaves. `biomes` lists where they may appear; `biomes_except` lists where they may not. Leave both out for anywhere. The biomes are `forest`, `desert`, `moor`, `tundra`, `sky_islands` and `large_caves`. |
| `night` | `light_max`, `distance`, `cap`, `cap_radius`, `fade_by_day`, `chance` | Once a second at night, one may appear `distance` (`{least, most}` blocks) from the player. It appears on solid ground no lighter than `light_max` (0â€“15), with room for its height, while fewer than `cap` of them are within `cap_radius` blocks. If `fade_by_day` is true, those the daylight reaches fade away when the day comes (not those in a cave). `chance` (0â€“1, default 1) is how often it tries at all: with 0.5, only half the seconds, so it appears half as often. |
| `dark` | `light_max`, `distance`, `cap`, `cap_radius`, `chance` | The same, at any time of day, where the light (sky and block light, whatever the time) is no more than `light_max`: with 0, anywhere no light reaches at all, such as a cave. It looks up and down from the player's height for such ground. |

The placeholder skins come from these files: `python gen_mob_skins.py` repaints `assets/texture/<ns>/<skin>.png` for every mob.
return {
  radius = 0.45,
  height = 1.35,
  walk_speed = 1.1,
  health = 10,
  drops = {
    {
      item = "worldcraft:meat",
      count = {1, 2},
    },
